{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-341237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x)=cos\\,(\\frac{x}{2})$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+4\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-341237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\nf(x+4\\pi )=f((x+2\\pi )+2\\pi )=f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=2\\pi .$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2\\pi ) & =f(0+2\\pi )=f(0)=F(0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\nF(2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. This is exactly the definition of $2\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-341237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-341237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-341237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-341237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x)=x$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,3)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+6)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3)$\n- $f(x+1)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+6)=f((x+3)+3)=f(x+3)=f(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true by definition of the periodic extension.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+1)=f(3)=F(3)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-340510" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340510" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340510" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-341232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for all $x\\in R$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-341232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f$ is the $\\pi /2$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Using the periodicity of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, any shift by a multiple of its period returns the same value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f((x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides equal $f(x),$ the statement is true for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-341232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-341232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-341232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-341232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-338489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-338489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f$ is $\\pi $-periodic, shifting by $2\\pi $ does not change the value of $f,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f((x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\pi )=f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does not always equal $f(x).$ For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=F(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\\ne f(x)$ for $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-338489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-338489" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-338489" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-338489" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-338490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-338490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =f((x-\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =f((x-2\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-338490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-338490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-338490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-338490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340681" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340681" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-7\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(-1-(-1))-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340681" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-340681" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340681" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340681" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340681" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340681" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "are not orthogonal"] ["text" "are orthogonal"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340681" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "are orthogonal"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(1-1)+\\frac{1}{6}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340369" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-340369" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340369" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340369" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340369" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340369" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "are not orthogonal"] ["text" "are orthogonal"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340369" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "are orthogonal"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-340528" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340528" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340528" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340528" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340528" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "are not orthogonal"] ["text" "are orthogonal"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340528" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "are orthogonal"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-6\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $g(x)=1$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-340669" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340669" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340669" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340669" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340669" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "are not orthogonal"] ["text" "are orthogonal"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340669" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "are orthogonal"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-0)-\\frac{1}{16}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340677" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-340677" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340677" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340677" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340677" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340677" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "are not orthogonal"] ["text" "are orthogonal"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340677" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "are orthogonal"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}+5x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{6}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{6}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{24}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-12\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & ={a}_{6}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{6} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{6} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-340638" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340638" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}"] ["math" "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}"] ["math" "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}"] ["math" "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)⟩}"] ["math" "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)⟩}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340638" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340638" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340638" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340638" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\pi }"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340638" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340638" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340638" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}+6x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{3}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={b}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{3} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-340648" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340648" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}"] ["math" "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}"] ["math" "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}"