52656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340647" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340647" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340647" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340647" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340647" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-6x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{4}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{4} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5b612f4e7d2cd0cf194f6754b23adcc90d2e2bcc76264c9d1123882c0b35bf0"} {"question" "q-340662" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340662" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}"] ["math" "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}"] ["math" "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}"] ["math" "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}"] ["math" "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340662" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340662" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340662" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340662" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340662" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340662" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "q-340662" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d3e09bc39049edf795ead1c96fe0cc8b3d69b41df165436f806d13513ef9149"} {"question" "e-21787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x) = \\cos(2x)$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f(x) = F(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/example-21787.json" "file_sha256" "044bd79e4e5b73c05d2e29a81f8ecf5e67c8e1add4eae911cbb47d01181a4270"} {"question" "e-21787" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in[0,\\pi).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi)=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi$-periodic, because for any $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=f(0)=F(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\neq f(x)$ for $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/example-21787.json" "file_sha256" "044bd79e4e5b73c05d2e29a81f8ecf5e67c8e1add4eae911cbb47d01181a4270"} {"question" "e-21788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) g(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ defined above, compute $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(6x), \\cos(4x) \\rangle$ and determine if functions $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/example-21788.json" "file_sha256" "f2fc9e5674e1b647b2a3c72256a00b7a599eeca2b750c159413c9fb4d3e70a32"} {"question" "e-21788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\sin(6x)\\cos(4x)\n=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\sin(10x)+\\sin(2x)\\big).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{4}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/example-21788.json" "file_sha256" "f2fc9e5674e1b647b2a3c72256a00b7a599eeca2b750c159413c9fb4d3e70a32"} {"question" "e-21789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $F(x) = 2x^2+5$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ to $x\\in \\mathbb R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx)\\right).\n$$\n\nDetermine the integral expression for the coefficient $a_4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/example-21789.json" "file_sha256" "11632d6ed7082e44ac648e23dcc467ed9f84bd15cffd36f501c3c82218e702bf"} {"question" "e-21789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left( a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0,$ $a_n,$ and $b_n$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\cos(4x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1, \\sin(x), \\cos(x), \\sin(2x), \\cos(2x), \\ldots$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\na_4 \\left\\langle \\cos(4x), \\cos(4x) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={a}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2{x}^{2}+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052656/example-21789.json" "file_sha256" "11632d6ed7082e44ac648e23dcc467ed9f84bd15cffd36f501c3c82218e702bf"}] "usage" {"e-21788" [] "q-340418" [] "e-21789" [] "e-21787" [] "q-338489" []} "basis" 1779 "retractions" [[17592186167203 :question/problem "Let $F(x) = \\sin (2x)$ for $x ∈ R$. Suppose $f(x)$ is the $π$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $[MATH: x ∈ [0, π)]$ to $x ∈ R$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x + π) = f(x)$ for all $x ∈ R$\n2. $f(x) = F(x)$ for all $x ∈ R$\n3. $f(x + \\frac{5π}{2}) = f(x)$ for all $x ∈ R$"] [17592186167220 :question/problem "Let $F(x) = x^{2}$ for $x ∈ R$, and let $f(x)$ be the $2π$-periodic extension of $F$ from $[MATH: x ∈ [- π, π)]$ to $x ∈ R$. The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx))$.\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient $a_{5}$ below, where $⟨, ⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[MATH: [- π, π).]$\n\n$(⟨\\sin (5x), \\sin (5x)⟩)/(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}"] [17592186166494 :question/problem "Let $F(x) = \\cos(2x)$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R$. Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $[MATH: x \\in [0,\\pi)]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x+\\pi) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n2. $f(x) = F(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n3. $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186166494 :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$, we have\n\n- $f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, and\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $[MATH: x\\in[0,\\pi).]$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi$-periodicity:\n$f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$\nfor all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi$-periodic, because for any $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, we have\n$F(x + π) = \\cos (2(x + π)) = \\cos (2x + 2π) = \\cos (2x) = F(x)$.\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0$. Then,\n\n$$\nf(x + \\frac{π}{2}) = f(\\frac{π}{2}) = F(\\frac{π}{2}) = \\cos (π) =-1\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\nf(x) = f(0) = F(0)\\cos (0) = 1\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nf\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\neq f(x)\n$$\n\nfor $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."] [17592186166495 :question/problem "$\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) g(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x$\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $[MATH: x\\in [0,2\\pi)]$ defined above, compute $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(6x), \\cos(4x) \\rangle$ and determine if functions $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product."] [17592186166495 :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\sin(6x)\\cos(4x) =\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\sin(10x)+\\sin(2x)\\big)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨\\sin (6x), \\cos (4x)⟩ &= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\sin (6x)\\cos (4x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (10x) + \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2x)dx \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{1}{20}\\cos (10x) - \\frac{1}{4}\\cos (2x)]_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{20}(\\cos (20π) - \\cos (0)) - \\frac{1}{4}(\\cos (4π) - \\cos (0)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{20}(1 - 1) - \\frac{1}{4}(1 - 1) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sin(6x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\cos(4x)\n$$\n\n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."] [17592186166496 :question/problem "Let $F(x) = 2x^2+5$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R$, and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $[MATH: x\\in [0,2\\pi)]$ to $x\\in \\mathbb R$. The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n$f(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx)\\right)$.\nDetermine the integral expression for the coefficient $a_4$."] [17592186166496 :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $[MATH: x\\in [0,2\\pi)]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $[MATH: x\\in [0,2\\pi),]$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0$, $a_n$, and $b_n$ are constants to be determined.\n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\cos(4x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(x), \\cos (4x)⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx)), \\cos (4x)⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨\\frac{a_{0}}{2}, \\cos (4x)⟩ + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)⟨a_{n}\\cos (nx), \\cos (4x)⟩ + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)⟨b_{n}\\sin (nx), \\cos (4x)⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{0}}{2}⟨1, \\cos (4x)⟩ + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}⟨\\cos (nx), \\cos (4x)⟩ + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}⟨\\sin (nx), \\cos (4x)⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1, \\sin(x), \\cos(x), \\sin(2x), \\cos(2x), \\ldots$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\na_4 \\left\\langle \\cos(4x), \\cos(4x) \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨\\cos (4x), \\cos (4x)⟩ &= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\cos^{2} (4x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (8x)dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{16}\\sin (8x)]_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2π - 0) - \\frac{1}{16}(\\sin (16π) - \\sin (0)) \\\\\n&= π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(x), \\cos (4x)⟩ &= a_{4}⟨\\cos (4x), \\cos (4x)⟩ \\\\\na_{4} &= (⟨f(x), \\cos (4x)⟩)/(⟨\\cos (4x), \\cos (4x)⟩) \\\\\na_{4} &= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{0}^{2π}f(x)\\cos (4x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π} \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2π}(2x^{2} + 5)\\cos (4x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186164737 17592186164738 17592186164739 17592186164740 17592186164741 17592186164869 17592186164870 17592186164871 17592186164872 17592186164873 17592186164875 17592186164876 17592186164877 17592186164878 17592186164879 17592186164880 17592186164882 17592186164883 17592186164884 17592186164885 17592186164886] "immutable_fields" [[17592186164736 :answer-field/correct] [17592186164736 :answer-field/id] [17592186164736 :answer-field/key] [17592186164736 :answer-field/type] [17592186164868 :answer-field/correct] [17592186164868 :answer-field/id] [17592186164868 :answer-field/key] [17592186164868 :answer-field/type] [17592186164874 :answer-field/correct] [17592186164874 :answer-field/id] [17592186164874 :answer-field/key] [17592186164874 :answer-field/type] [17592186164881 :answer-field/correct] [17592186164881 :answer-field/id] [17592186164881 :answer-field/key] [17592186164881 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
